    {
      "type": "row",
      "title": "What do these numbers mean?",
      "id": 14,
      "collapsed": true,
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 1,
        "w": 24,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 39
      },
      "panels": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "title": "",
          "id": 10,
          "gridPos": {
            "h": 30,
            "w": 24,
            "x": 0,
            "y": 40
          },
          "options": {
            "mode": "markdown",
            "content": "### How SMART works\n\nEvery disk keeps a set of health counters, its **SMART attributes**. Each has:\n\n- a **raw** value: the real counter, e.g. \"8 reallocated sectors\". Its format is up to the vendor.\n- a **normalized** value (usually 100–253 when healthy) that drops towards a **threshold** the vendor set. When any normalized value crosses its threshold, the drive's overall verdict becomes **FAILED** (the *SMART* column).\n\nThe overall verdict is conservative: drives often fail *before* it trips. That is why the raw counters below are watched individually.\n\n### Is a drive failing? (the health table)\n\n| Column | What it counts | How worried to be |\n|---|---|---|\n| **197 Pending** | Sectors the disk could not read, waiting to be remapped | **Any value > 0: plan a replacement.** The best single predictor of failure. Alert: critical. |\n| **198 Offline unc.** | Sectors the disk's own background scan could not read | Same as 197. Alert: critical. |\n| **5 Reallocated** | Bad sectors already replaced by spares | A few that stay put is survivable. **Growing** = the surface is degrading. Alert when it grows. |\n| **187 Uncorrect.** | Reads the disk's error correction could not fix | btrfs repairs the data from the other copy, but the disk is unreliable. Alert when it grows. |\n| **188 Timeouts** | Commands that timed out | Often a cable or power issue rather than the disk. Seagate packs three counters into this one; the table shows the total (low 16 bits). Alert when it grows. |\n| **199 CRC (cable)** | Data corrupted between disk and controller | The **cable or port**, not the disk: reseat or replace it. |\n| **Failed self-tests** | Self-tests (short every night, long on the 15th, run by smartd) that hit an error | A failed read test means bad media. Alert: critical. |\n| **NVMe media err.** | Uncorrectable errors on an NVMe drive | Alert when it grows. |\n\nBackblaze's studies of their drive fleet found that most failed drives showed a non-zero value in one of **5, 187, 188, 197, 198** beforehand, while very few healthy drives did. That is why those are the ones watched.\n\n**Seagate quirk, ignore these:** attributes **1** (Raw_Read_Error_Rate), **7** (Seek_Error_Rate) and **195** (Hardware_ECC_Recovered) show huge, ever-growing raw values on Seagate drives. They pack an *operation count* in with the error count, so the number is meaningless on its own. This is normal and is deliberately not shown or alerted on.\n\n### How much life is left?\n\n- **NVMe/SSD: measurable.** Flash wears out with writes. *NVMe endurance used* is the drive's own estimate of how much of its rated write endurance is gone, and *projected wear-out* extrapolates from the last 30 days.\n- **Hard drives: no tool can give a date.** They fail mechanically, not from wear. The best estimate combines:\n  1. **Age.** Failure rates stay low for years, then rise after about 5 (the \"bathtub curve\").\n  2. **The model's track record.** Backblaze publishes annual failure rates per drive model every quarter: <https://www.backblaze.com/cloud-storage/resources/hard-drive-test-data>. Look up the *Model* column there.\n  3. **The trend of the counters above.** A drive with all zeros at 6 years is in better shape than one with growing pending sectors at 2.\n\n### btrfs device errors\n\nbtrfs checksums all data, so it notices corruption SMART does not report (and the reverse). Its counters are per LUKS device (`crypt-bulk-0`…), not per disk: `cryptsetup status <name>` on the host shows which disk sits under each. With RAID1, btrfs repairs a bad copy from the good one, so an error is a warning sign, not data loss.\n\nWhat to do when a disk needs replacing: *Replacing a failed disk* in `docs/hypervisor/overview.md`.",
            "code": {
              "language": "plaintext",
              "showLineNumbers": false,
              "showMiniMap": false
            }
          }
        }
      ]
    }
